# ---- This project ----
# Headless build logs from the initial project setup - regenerable, not source.
_setup_logs/
# Screenshot scratch: the MCP camera tool writes here during visual checks.
Captures/
[Aa]ssets/[Ss]creenshots/
[Aa]ssets/[Ss]creenshots.meta

# The scene is generated, not authored. SceneBuilder.Build() is the source of truth for
# every object in it, and Unity assigns fresh fileIDs to all ~1,700 of them on each rebuild -
# so a one-line change to a script produced an 11,000-line scene diff with no semantic content,
# and any merge of two rebuilds was a guaranteed conflict over noise. Run the build to get it:
#   Unity.exe -batchmode -nographics -projectPath . -executeMethod IterationRoom.EditorTools.SceneBuilder.Build -quit
# The .meta is deliberately NOT ignored: it pins the scene's GUID, which
# ProjectSettings/EditorBuildSettings.asset stores. Drop it and every clone regenerates a
# different GUID and dirties that file.
[Aa]ssets/[Ss]cenes/*.unity

# BAKE OUTPUT: the Adaptive Probe Volume data, and the lighting/reflection assets beside it.
#
# Unity files a scene's baked probe data in a folder named after the scene, next to the scene
# (`ProbeVolumeBakingSet.GetDirectory`), so these appeared the day the bake first worked - about
# 30MB of binary cell data across the four scenes, rewritten WHOLE by every bake.
#
# **Ignored for the same reason the scenes above are, and it is the stronger case of the two.** A
# scene is 11,000 lines of churn; this is 30MB of opaque binary that no diff can say anything about,
# regenerated by a menu item in about four minutes.
#
# **The argument for committing it is real, and it was weighed.** Probe data is keyed by world
# position and by the scene GUID, which the tracked `.meta` pins, and `SceneBuilder` is deterministic
# - so a fresh clone that runs `Build()` would find committed probe data still valid and get bounce
# light without baking. If the lighting is ever settled and that four minutes starts to matter, this
# block is what to remove. While the values are still moving, it is 30MB of churn per pass.
[Aa]ssets/[Ss]cenes/*/
[Aa]ssets/[Ss]cenes/*.meta
!/[Aa]ssets/[Ss]cenes/*.unity.meta
[Aa]ssets/[Ss]ettings/ProbeVolumes/
[Aa]ssets/[Ss]ettings/ProbeVolumes.meta

# **THE GHOST ANIMATOR IS BUILD OUTPUT, FOR THE SAME REASON THE SCENES ARE.**
# `SceneBuilder.GhostAnimatorController()` clears every state and rebuilds Walk/Idle/Swing on each
# run - adding is not idempotent, so a retried build would otherwise stack duplicates - and Unity
# issues fresh fileIDs each time. The result is a permanent 56-line diff in which nothing has
# changed: the states, the clips and the default are identical, only the IDs move. It was dirty in
# every working tree that had ever built, which is noise that teaches you to ignore `git status`.
#
# The `.meta` stays tracked, exactly as the scenes' do, so the GUID other assets reference is pinned.
# `Ghost.prefab` beside it is also generated but does not churn, so it stays committed - if it ever
# starts showing the same no-op diff, it belongs here too.
[Aa]ssets/[Pp]refabs/GhostAnimator.controller

# Same failure, different asset: `TreeBark_Cut.mat` carries two MonoBehaviour sub-assets (one more
# than any other generated material) and Unity renumbers both on every rebuild. Content identical,
# fourteen lines of diff, every time. Its `.meta` stays tracked for the GUID.
[Aa]ssets/[Mm]aterials/TreeBark_Cut.mat

# **THE REFLECTION CUBEMAPS ARE BAKED BY EVERY BUILD.** `BakeReflectionProbes()` runs last in
# `Build()` and re-renders all of them, so twenty-one binary files show as modified after a build
# that changed nothing - proven by a build whose only edit was to this file. They are the same
# category as the scenes: expensive-looking output that is cheap to regenerate and impossible to
# review in a diff.
#
# The `.meta` files stay tracked. The probes are `Custom` mode with `customBakedTexture` pointing at
# these by GUID, so pinning the GUID is what lets a fresh clone wire up correctly the moment it
# builds. Without a build there are no cubemaps and every glossy surface reflects nothing - which is
# exactly the bargain the scenes already make.
[Aa]ssets/[Tt]extures/*_Reflection.exr

# What the cross-scene reference check writes on every build. Build output about build output -
# it says what the CURRENT scenes look like, so a committed copy would only ever be someone else's.
cross-scene-report.txt

# Screenshots dropped into docs/ while diagnosing something. They are the raw material for a
# `gotchas.md` entry, not the entry - once the finding is written up the picture has done its job.
docs/*.png
docs/*.jpg

# Byte-cache from the Tools/ generator scripts.
__pycache__/

# Burst drops its generated WebGL plugin at the project root during a player build.
# Build output, not source - the same category as Build/.
/Data/

# Trailer captures. Recorder writes 1080p60 MP4 here; none of it belongs in git - and Unity's
# Recorder sometimes drops one at the project root instead, so catch those too.
/Recordings/
# Screen recordings, wherever they are dropped. This was `/*.mp4` - root only - and a 277MB
# capture sitting in `docs/` was one `git add .` away from being in this history for good.
*.mp4

# The TTS generator's environment - torch and the MeloTTS packs, ~3GB.
# Created by hand, never by the build; see the header of Tools/generate_narration.py.
.venv-tts/
Tools/__pycache__/
.venv-qwen/

# Voice audition clips - regenerable from Tools/generate_narration.py, and nothing reads them.
docs/voice-samples/

